           3. Semidefinite Programming bounds on quantum codes
  We sketch the semidefinite programming (SDP) bounds as derived by Ref. [MNH25,
Theorem 22]. The formulation is inspired by the fact that the Lovász theta number upper
bounds classical codes [Lov79], and corresponds to an intermediate level of a complete
hierarchy presented in Ref. [MNH25, Theorem 7].
  Below, we present three SDP formulations: the main SDP, a Lovász type SDP, and the
symmetry-reduced version of the main SDP. For the numerical results in Section 4, we
only use the symmetry-reduced SDP.

3.1. Main SDP. The key idea is the following: Let ϱ = Π/K be a state proportional to
the projector of a code subspace Π. Let Γ be a matrix which is indexed by all elements
of the n-qubit Pauli basis En , with entries determined by the pair Ea , Eb ∈ En as
(17)                           Γab (ϱ) = tr(Ea† ϱ) tr(Eb ϱ) tr(Ea Eb† ϱ) .
By convention, we set E0 = 1 and thus Γ00 (ϱ) = 1. One can show that Γ(ϱ) is positive
semidefinite, because it can be constructed as a moment matrix from ϱ ⪰ 0. Importantly,
the quantum weight enumerators Aj [Eq. (4)] can be obtained from Γ by summing over
its diagonal elements,
